# OPTY.bat must be stored AND served with CRLF line endings.
#
# Why: cmd.exe resolves the return address of `call :label` (and `goto :eof`)
# as a byte offset into the file. That bookkeeping assumes 2-byte CRLF line
# endings. With LF-only endings the offset drifts, and execution can resume in
# the middle of an unrelated section instead of after the call. It is not
# deterministic - it depends where each label lands - which makes it worse:
# the script mostly works, then derails deep in a long run.
#
# `text eol=crlf` was NOT enough: it normalises the blob to LF *in the repo*
# and only converts on checkout, so raw.githubusercontent.com and the
# "Download raw file" button still hand out LF.
# `-text` disables normalisation entirely, so the CRLF bytes are stored as-is
# and every download path (raw, blob, archive, release asset) is byte-identical.
#
# After changing this file:  git add --renormalize .   (then commit)
# Verify:  git show HEAD:OPTY.bat | (byte-count CR)  -> must be non-zero
*.bat   -text
*.cmd   -text

# Same reasoning for the PowerShell release tooling (run on Windows only).
*.ps1   -text

# Plain text, let Git handle it normally.
*.md    text
